[0.0, "o", "\u001b[?25l"]
[0.0, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Work out whether switching from my gas boiler to a heat pump would save me money, as a Markdown file\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[3.267, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mRan 1 shell command\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Bash(ls -la \"<workspace>\" | head -50)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[29.728, "o", "\u001b[1A\u001b[2K\u001b[1A\u001b[2K\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mRan 1 shell command, wrote 1 file\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Write(<workspace>/heat-pump-vs-gas-boiler.md)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[33.917, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I wrote the analysis to `<workspace>/heat-pump-vs-gas-boiler.md`. It's based on assumed figures, because the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  folder had no bills or home details. The prices are rough UK estimates in £ and weren't looked up, so check\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  them against current rates.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  On those assumptions (12,000 kWh of gas a year, an 85%-efficient boiler, a typical semi-detached home):\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Standard electricity tariff (25p/kWh):** running costs come out about even. A heat pump needs an\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  efficiency (SCOP) of about 3.4 to break even. At a typical SCOP of 3.2 it costs about £40 a year more to\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  run. Dropping the gas standing charge turns that into roughly £70 a year saved.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Heat-pump or time-of-use tariff (18p/kWh):** it saves about £180–£380 a year, depending on the heat\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  pump's efficiency and whether you disconnect gas.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Payback:** about 15–20 years on running costs alone, after a £7,500 grant. It's shorter if your boiler\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  is due for replacement anyway.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The file also has the break-even formula and a step-by-step way to rerun it with your own numbers.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  For a real answer, I need:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Your annual gas kWh.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Your gas and electricity unit rates and the gas standing charge.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Your country.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Any installer quote, especially its predicted SCOP.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  With those I can update the file.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[34.036, "o", "\r\n\u001b[33m⚑ Stop hook: Before you finish:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  ⎿  … +286 lines of instructions\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[37.29, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mRan 1 shell command\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Bash(cd \"<workspace>\" && mkdir -p .ambition/first && cp heat-pump-vs-gas-boiler.md .ambition/first/ &…\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[40.308, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I've saved a copy of the file in `.ambition/first/`. There's no `.gitignore` in this folder, so I didn't\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  change one.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Do you want to stop here or push it further?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Stop here:** keep the file as it is.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Push it further:** I'd go after three things.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    1. **Your own numbers.** I'd ask about your home, tariffs and any installer quote, then work out a real\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  saving and payback instead of an illustrative one.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    2. **A runnable calculator.** This would be a small script or spreadsheet instead of hand-worked tables,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  with a sensitivity check on SCOP, tariff and price changes.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    3. **Checked prices and grants.** I'd look up current gas and electricity rates and grant rules instead of\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  using my rough estimates.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    The cost is modest compared with what's been spent so far: a few short questions from me, then one or two\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  more rounds of work.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[44.53, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Push it further.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[47.202, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ▸ Step 2/8 · Altitude\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  First question: what is this analysis for, and what should happen once it's done?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  1. **Decide whether to get a heat pump.** You want a clear yes or no, based on your own house.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  2. **Prepare for installer quotes.** You want to know what SCOP and tariff a quote needs to beat to be worth\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  it.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  3. **Compare with other options.** For example, replacing the boiler like-for-like, or adding solar or a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  battery.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  4. **Something else.** Tell me what.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[51.819, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Option 1: I want to decide whether to get a heat pump. I'd like a clear yes or no for my own house, with the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  reasoning behind it, so I can act on it rather than just have numbers. If it's close, tell me what would tip\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  it either way.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[55.723, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ Second question: what would make the result unmistakably excellent? Pick the closest, or describe your own.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  1. **A firm verdict with a margin.** It says yes, no or too close, and states how confident it is. It also\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  lists the 2–3 facts that would flip the answer, for example \"yes if your quote's SCOP is 3.4 or higher\".\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  2. **Verdict plus a plan.** It's the same as option 1, plus the next steps if the answer is yes (what to ask\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  installers, which grant to apply for, which tariff to switch to).\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  3. **Verdict plus a 10–15 year cash-flow view.** It's the same as option 1, with cumulative cost against\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  keeping the boiler, including boiler replacement and price scenarios.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  4. **Something else.** Tell me what.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I'd also hold the file to a reading standard: one-page answer first, working underneath, no jargon without a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  plain explanation. Tell me if you'd weigh that differently.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[61.086, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Option 3 is closest, but with the verdict first. I want a clear yes, no or too close, with how confident you\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  are, and the few facts that would flip it. Then underneath, the 10–15 year cost comparison against keeping\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  the boiler, including when the boiler would need replacing. I'm trying to decide whether to spend the money,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  so the long-run picture matters more than a to-do list.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  Your reading standard is fine: a one-page answer first, working underneath, and plain explanations of any\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  jargon.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[64.846, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ Third question: what could check this other than me? Pick what you can get, or several.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  1. **Your real bills and an installer quote.** I'd compare my modelled gas use against your actual meter\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  readings. The quote's heat-loss figure and predicted SCOP would replace my assumptions.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  2. **Published reference data.** This means the official Ofgem price cap and typical-use figures, and trial\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  results from field studies of UK heat pumps. I'd check my numbers against these.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  3. **A runnable model.** A script that recomputes every figure in the file from a single set of inputs. You\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  could change an input and see the verdict move, and it would let me test the verdict against its own\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  scenarios.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  4. **Something else.** Tell me what.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I'd suggest 2 and 3 together, plus 1 if you can share your bills.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[70.257, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ I'd go with 2 and 3 together. Use the official Ofgem price cap figures and the UK field-trial results for\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  heat pump efficiency, and give me a small script that recomputes every number in the Markdown file from one\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  set of inputs, so I can change things like my gas use or electricity price and see whether the verdict\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  changes. I don't have an installer quote yet, and I can't easily dig out my bills right now, so go with\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  typical figures for a UK home and flag clearly which numbers are assumptions.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[74.248, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mLoaded 1 tool\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  ToolSearch({\"query\": \"select:WebSearch,WebFetch\", \"max_results\": 2})\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[82.322, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ Web search and fetch are now available, so no access is missing. I'll fetch the Ofgem and field-trial\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  figures once you've confirmed this.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ```\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  You asked for:   Would switching from my gas boiler to a heat pump save me money, as a Markdown file\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Raises all of:   - \"Would it save money\": a firm yes / no / too close verdict for a typical UK home,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     with stated confidence, instead of a table of scenarios.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - \"As a Markdown file\": still one readable .md file, not an app or a spreadsheet.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Genre bar:       A decision memo a non-expert can act on. It leads with the answer on one page and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   puts the working underneath. Jargon (SCOP, standing charge, flow temperature) is\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   explained in plain words. Assumptions are visibly flagged, and the reader can\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   trust it without a calculator.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Aiming at:       You deciding whether to spend the money, because the long-run picture\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   matters more than a to-do list.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Excellent means: 1. Page one: verdict (yes / no / too close), confidence, and the 2–3 facts that\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      would flip it, each with a threshold (e.g. \"yes if SCOP ≥ X\").\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   2. A 10–15 year cumulative cost comparison against keeping the boiler,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      including when the boiler would need replacing, plus price scenarios.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   3. Inputs from Ofgem's price cap and UK field-trial SCOP data, with sources\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      cited. Every assumption is labelled as one.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   4. A small script that recomputes every number in the file from one set of\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      inputs, so changing gas use or electricity price shows whether the verdict\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                      changes.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Proven by:       - The script regenerates every figure in the file with no mismatches.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - Inputs are cross-checked against the Ofgem cap and the field-trial results.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - The verdict is tested against its own scenarios, to show how stable it is.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   - I read the final file through as a first-time reader, against the first\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                     version. It must be at least as clear and readable as that version.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Needs access to: Web (now loaded). Not available: your bills or an installer quote, so typical\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m                   UK figures are used and flagged.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Mode:            build · answerer: you · reviewer: none (no step-by-step review requested)\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ```\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Is this right? Reply \"confirmed\", or tell me what to change.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[87.701, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m❯ Confirmed. One note: assume a typical 3-bed semi in England on the current Ofgem price cap, with an air-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  source heat pump costing about £7,500 after the Boiler Upgrade Scheme grant. Please keep it to a single .md\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  file, with the verdict up top and the script's output pasted in at the bottom. Go ahead and finish without\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[97m  stopping.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[91.362, "o", "\r\n\u001b[32m⏺\u001b[0m \u001b[1mRan 1 shell command\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[90m  ⎿  Bash(cd \"<workspace>\" && mkdir -p .ambition/steps/2 && cp heat-pump-vs-gas-boiler.md .ambition/steps/…\u001b[0m\r\n"]
