[351.367, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ The heat pump doesn't pay for itself on the standard price cap, and on a heat-pump tariff it's too close to\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  call. It's written up in `<workspace>/heat-pump-vs-gas-boiler.md`, with the verdict at the top. The model\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  behind it is `heatpump_model.py` in the same folder, and the script's output is pasted at the bottom of the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Markdown file, as you asked.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Verdict:** on a standard tariff the answer is no, with moderate confidence. The heat pump finished cheaper\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  in 6 of 54 stress scenarios, so 89% agree on \"no\". On a heat-pump tariff it's too close: after 15 years it's\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  £46 worse off. Most of the cost is the up-front £7,500, which is why it doesn't pay back. In year 1 the heat\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  pump's running costs are only £71 lower than the boiler's on a standard tariff, or £234 lower on a heat-pump\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  tariff.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Over 15 years** (heat pump installed now, versus keeping the boiler and replacing it in year 5):\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Tariff | Heat pump vs keeping the boiler |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  |---|---|\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Standard (price cap) | £3,077 worse off |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  | Heat-pump tariff | £46 worse off |\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What would flip it**, each worked out in the file:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Efficiency (SCOP):** a predicted SCOP of about 4.0 on a standard tariff, or about 3.0 on a heat-pump\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  tariff.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Electricity price:** a heat-pump tariff blended at 19.9p/kWh or lower.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Boiler:** a boiler that fails soon tips a heat-pump tariff to about £820 ahead. A boiler that never\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  needs replacing in the period makes it worse.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Gas use:** 12,000 kWh a year instead of 9,500, on a heat-pump tariff, makes it about £940 ahead.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Checks:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Ofgem bill:** the Ofgem prices and typical use reproduce Ofgem's published £1,723 typical bill to within\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  £1.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Regeneration:** `--set` changes any input, `--write` rebuilds the file, and `--check` confirms the file\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  matches the model. Changing the inputs does change the verdict.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Which numbers are assumptions:** they're flagged in the file's assumptions table. The main ones are:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - A SCOP of 3.0, a 20p blended heat-pump tariff, and a £3,000 replacement boiler.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - The boiler's age, and service costs.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Both fuels rising 3% a year.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I used 9,500 kWh of gas a year, which is Ofgem's new typical figure, so it may understate a 3-bed semi. I\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  took that figure from Energy UK's summary, not Ofgem's own page, which I couldn't open.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **What I couldn't confirm:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Electricity VAT:** Ofgem's page lists no VAT on electricity until March 2027, and I couldn't find what\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  happens after. The base case ignores a return of VAT, and one sensitivity row models it.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Efficiency data:** the 2.8 median is from interim results of the Electrification of Heat trial, because\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I found no final report.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Grant end date:** I didn't confirm when the grant ends.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Your first version is untouched in `.ambition/first/`, as the hook asked. The other pipeline steps (depth,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  challenge, range and so on) aren't run, since you asked me to finish in one go.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
