[607.402, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ **Step 4 report: Challenge**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Did:** I tested each assumption against real material, riskiest first, and added a \"What I checked against\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  evidence\" section to the file. It lists each assumption, what the evidence says, and what changed.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Found:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **The blind spot:** the base case treated a temporary electricity VAT holiday as permanent. Ofgem's page\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  says VAT is removed only from October 2026 to 31 March 2027, so the 26.32p rate I used is VAT-free. With VAT\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the price is 27.64p, and electricity costs 3.5 times as much as gas per kWh, not 3.3. That quietly flattered\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the heat pump for 15 years.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Gas use:** the government's household energy dataset (NEED) puts the median at 10,000 kWh, and the semi-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  detached mean at about 11,700 kWh. My 9,500 was too low.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Boiler cost:** the typical replacement is £2,000–£3,500, so my £3,000 was a little high.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Heat-pump tariff price:** my flat 20p was a guess. I replaced it with the tariff's own published\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  structure. The price depends on when you run the heat pump, from about 18p to 24p.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Your £7,500 price:** published data suggests the market median is about £5,500 net, so your figure is\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  above it. At £5,500 the heat-pump-tariff case flips to about £500 ahead, so get quotes.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Grant:** confirmed at £7,500, extended to 2028, with no requirement to disconnect gas.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Not tested:** I couldn't test the efficiency spread against household-level data. I also found no source\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  for future fuel prices or the cost of disconnecting gas. The first two are the largest drivers, and the file\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  says so.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Changed course:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Base inputs:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - electricity VAT is 5%\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - gas use is 10,500 kWh\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - the replacement boiler costs £2,500\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m    - the heat-pump tariff is 80% of the standard rate\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Verdict labels:** the heat pump on a heat-pump tariff is now £1,171 worse off on the point estimate,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  which would read as \"no\". The simulation's median is near break-even, though. Where the two disagree, the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  label now says \"too close\".\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Overall verdict:** it's the same as before, with the numbers now better grounded. On a standard tariff\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  it's no, with high confidence (94% of 5,000 draws). On a heat-pump tariff it's too close. That's about\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  £4,100 worse off on a standard tariff and £1,170 worse off on a heat-pump tariff.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Self-test:** all four checks still pass, and I saved a copy to `.ambition/steps/4/`.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
