[951.69, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ ▸ Step 7/8 · Stretch\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The brief:** a yes / no / too-close verdict for a typical English 3-bed semi, in one Markdown file, with a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  script that recomputes it. That's done and passing.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **The stretch target:** your own verdict from three numbers, in one command and under about 10 seconds, with\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  no knowledge of the model. The three numbers are your gas kWh (or m³) from your bill, the SCOP an installer\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  predicts, and their price after the grant. That's several times easier than today's list of `--set` options,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and it works on the day a quote arrives.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Fallback:** the file as it stands, with `--set` for full control.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **Where it would likely break:**\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Gas meter units:** m³ versus kWh is the likeliest failure.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Inputs outside the model:** the three-number shortcut can't handle fixed deals or unusual homes.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Speed:** the 5,000-draw simulation might be too slow.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I'll measure the current speed first.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
