[1212.441, "o", "\r\n\u001b[97m⏺ I've finished all eight steps. Your own verdict now takes one command and about 0.1 seconds: `python3\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  heatpump_model.py --me --gas-kwh N --scop X --price P`.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  **On typical figures for a 3-bed semi in England, the heat pump doesn't save money.** On a standard tariff\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  it is about £4,100 worse off after 15 years, and the simulation agrees in 94% of 5,000 runs. On a heat-pump\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  tariff it is too close to call (about £1,150 worse off).\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ## You asked for → it became\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **\"Would a heat pump save me money?\"** became a yes / no / too-close verdict with confidence. It also\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  gives the efficiency, price and gas use at which it would flip, and a 15-year comparison that includes when\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  the boiler would need replacing.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **\"As a Markdown file\"** stayed one file, `heat-pump-vs-gas-boiler.md`, with the verdict first and the\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  script's output pasted at the bottom. A recompute script now sits beside it.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **\"My gas boiler\"** became a typical English 3-bed semi on the Ofgem price cap, as you specified. You can\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  swap in your own gas use, SCOP and price with `--me`.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ## Against the first version\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Better:** the first version had no verdict at the top, no source links, no regenerable numbers and only\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  two flagged assumptions. The final has all of those, plus tested thresholds, quote-checking grids, decisions\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  and next checks.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Gave up:** length. The file went from about 900 to about 4,200 words, though page one stays under 300\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  words and the rest is reference.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - **Check on both:** the same checks found the first version failing the verdict-first, sources and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  regenerable-numbers tests. The final passes all 84.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ## Outside check\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  `check_report.py` ran 84 checks and all pass. They include:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - An independent reimplementation of the cost model that matches to the penny across six input sets and both\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  tariffs.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - The pasted output matching a fresh run.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Ofgem's £1,723 typical bill reproduced within £1, and Nesta's published parity result.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Every source link loading.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - A cold read of the whole file.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  It caught a real bug in the break-even price, which is now fixed. Source checks also made me correct three\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  claims I had overstated.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ## Stretch\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  The target was a personal verdict from three numbers, in under 10 seconds. It reached about 0.1 seconds, and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  `--gas-m3` handles meter readings. It breaks on fixed-price deals, where you need `--set` to enter your own\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  rates, and I couldn't predict SCOP from flow temperature without data to check it against.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ## Locked in\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  In `<workspace>`:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - `heat-pump-vs-gas-boiler.md`: the deliverable.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - `heatpump_model.py`: the model that generates it.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - `check_report.py`: the independent checks.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - `CLAUDE.md`: how to rerun and what counts as done.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - `.claude/commands/heat-pump.md`: the `/heat-pump` command.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  To rerun everything, use `python3 heatpump_model.py --write heat-pump-vs-gas-boiler.md`, then `--check`,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  then `python3 -I check_report.py heat-pump-vs-gas-boiler.md heatpump_model.py`. A copy of each step is in\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  `.ambition/steps/`, and the first version is in `.ambition/first/`.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ## Assumptions made\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  I used your instruction to finish without stopping. The assumptions are flagged in the file's assumptions\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  table:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - 10,500 kWh of gas a year.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - SCOP 3.0.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - A heat-pump tariff at 80% of the standard rate.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - A £2,500 replacement boiler replaced in year 5.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - Both fuels rising 3% a year.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  - 5% VAT on electricity from April 2027.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ## Decisions\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  All three are in the file with owners and dates. The most urgent is the first.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  1. **Whether to buy.** The options are:\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m     - Keep the boiler and revisit when it fails.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m     - Collect 2–3 quotes first.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m     - Commit now.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m     Keeping the boiler costs nothing but the grant may end. Quotes turn the two biggest unknowns into facts.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Committing now stakes £7,500 on typical figures. I'd collect quotes, and default to keeping the boiler\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  unless a quote lands in a \"yes\" cell. You decide by 31 January 2027.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  2. **Tariff, if you proceed.** A standard tariff is simple but about £4,100 worse off. A heat-pump tariff is\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  cheaper only if the heat pump runs mostly in the cheap windows. I'd choose the heat-pump tariff, but only\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  with the installer's written plan for when the heat pump runs. You decide by 31 January 2027.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  3. **Disconnecting gas.** Disconnecting at once stops the standing charge (about £110 a year) but removes\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  your fallback. Keeping it costs about £1,300 over 15 years if kept for good. I'd keep it through the first\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  winter, then disconnect if the heat pump performs well. You decide by 31 March 2028, if installed in 2027.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Your first next check is to run `--me` with your real gas kWh from your last 12 months of bills, by 20\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  November 2026.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ## Metrics\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ```\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  Metrics since 2026-11-13T01:31:37Z (cost: /cost, or total_cost_usd in headless JSON)\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  total   in 166 · out 129.8k (think 54.0k) · cache read 10.75M · write 228.6k · hit 98% · 77 calls\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  model   sonnet-5-5: in 166 · out 129.8k (think 54.0k) · cache read 10.75M · write 228.6k · hit 98% · 77\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  calls\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[97m  ```\u001b[0m\r\n"]
